{"summary":"Reviewed input/disclosure.md and input/conversation.md (identical text), all eleven draft sections, draft/numerals.md, and the four figure briefs. Two workspace conditions limited part of the review and should be stated plainly: (a) figures/ contains only FIG-1.md through FIG-4.md — there are no rendered-*.png images and no review/figure-audit-evidence.json in this workspace, so no pixel, OCR, leader or section-line verification was possible; figure review was confined to agreement between each brief, numerals.md and the specification (the absent sheets are already covered by the mechanical "described figure has a drawing sheet" failure and are not re-reported here); (b) prior_art/INDEX.md lists "(none)" and the draft contains no [REF:...] citation, so the citation-fidelity check is vacuous — nothing is characterised that a source could contradict.

On the material checked, the draft is unusually faithful. Every claim limitation traces to an exact passage of the disclosure: the four stored per-session values, the allocation-interval subtraction of measured non-charging branch load and configurable safety reserve, minimum-sustaining-first then energy-deficit distribution, pilot send plus connector-sensor verification, the verification interval after a reduced pilot command, ordered one-at-a-time shedding until branch current is below the limit, the three reclosure conditions, the leakage-threshold welded-contactor record with inhibition of the other contactors and the local fault indicator, the network interface / signed local token / last accepted branch limit / offline continuation / non-start of new sessions, the tamper-evident meter sequence, and the service-input commissioning map confirmed at the sensing channel. Every numeral in numerals.md is used for one part only and appears in exactly one consistent role in the detailed description and in a figure brief; 100/102/104/106/108/118/120/140 on FIG. 1, 108/120/122/124/126/128/130 on FIG. 2, 106/110/112/114/116/132/134 on FIG. 3, 202-216 on FIG. 4, with no numeral labelling two different parts. The Brief Description of the Drawings matches what each brief depicts, and no figure is said to show something its brief omits. The abstract is 146 words. The claim-word warning is a false positive (the missing words are the token "claim" from dependent-claim references).

Three defects are reported: one added, self-contradicting statement about where the branch current limit comes from; one term ("staged reduction") used for two different steps; and one apparatus claim that operates an element it never recites. All three have local, source-supported fixes.","findings":[{"severity":"major","category":"disclosure_fidelity","title":"Detailed description says the branch current limit is received over the network interface, which the disclosure does not state and which contradicts the same section's own definition of that limit","where":"draft/08-detailed-description.md, paragraph at line 37 (and paragraph at line 1)","detail":"The disclosure confines the network interface to remote schedules and authorizations: "The controller can receive remote schedules and authorizations through a network interface, but it stores a signed local authorization token and the last accepted branch limit in nonvolatile memory." It never says the branch limit itself arrives over the network. The candidate adds that origin, and the addition collides with the candidate's own opening definition of the branch current limit as a physical property of the installation. As written the document gives the branch current limit two mutually exclusive sources — the conductor/overcurrent-device/supply rating in paragraph 1, and a value most recently received over the network in paragraph 37 — and the reader cannot tell which quantity claims 1, 9, 14 and 18 allocate against. Note that the summary (draft/06-summary.md line 9) and claims 9 and 18 stay neutral ("to hold a signed local authorization token and a last accepted branch limit in the nonvolatile memory"), so the unsupported assertion is local to this paragraph and can be removed without disturbing the claims. Paragraph 1's list of what fixes the limit ("by the overcurrent device protecting it and by the upstream supply capacity") is likewise not in the disclosure, which says only "one electrical branch whose allowable aggregate current is lower than the sum of the connector ratings"; the two passages together are what produce the contradiction.","evidence":"Paragraph 37: "The last accepted branch limit is the branch current limit most recently received and accepted through the network interface 110, retained so that a loss of the network does not leave the edge controller 106 without a limit to allocate against." Paragraph 1: "A branch conductor 102 supplies the installation and has a branch current limit, that is, an allowable aggregate current fixed by the rating of the branch conductor 102, by the overcurrent device protecting it and by the upstream supply capacity." Disclosure: "The controller can receive remote schedules and authorizations through a network interface, but it stores a signed local authorization token and the last accepted branch limit in nonvolatile memory."","fix":"In paragraph 37 delete the network origin and keep only the disclosed retention function: replace "The last accepted branch limit is the branch current limit most recently received and accepted through the network interface 110, retained so that a loss of the network does not leave the edge controller 106 without a limit to allocate against." with "The last accepted branch limit is the branch current limit most recently accepted by the edge controller 106, retained in the nonvolatile memory 112 so that a loss of the network does not leave the edge controller 106 without a limit to allocate against." In paragraph 1 replace "an allowable aggregate current fixed by the rating of the branch conductor 102, by the overcurrent device protecting it and by the upstream supply capacity" with "an allowable aggregate current of the branch conductor 102", matching the disclosure's wording."},{"severity":"minor","category":"terminology","title":""Staged reduction" names two different steps of FIG. 4","where":"draft/08-detailed-description.md, paragraphs at lines 25 and 27; draft/numerals.md line 28; draft/07-drawings.md line 7","detail":"The connector-level response at the connector verification step 208 is expressly labelled "the staged reduction of the allocation", while numeral 210 is separately defined in numerals.md and in the detailed description as "staged reduction step 210", a different step performing a different comparison (measured branch current against the branch current limit). The Brief Description of the Drawings uses the same phrase again for FIG. 4 as a whole. One name is therefore attached to two distinct numbered operations, and a reader following "the staged reduction" cannot tell whether step 208 or step 210 is meant. The disclosure uses the phrase once, for the pilot-signalling stage that precedes shedding ("staged reduction followed by ordered contactor shedding when branch current does not respond"), so the fix is to reserve the labelled name for step 210 and describe step 208's action as its first stage.","evidence":"Line 25: "This is the staged reduction of the allocation, and it is the first response of the edge controller 106 to a connector channel that is drawing more than it has been allowed." Line 27: "At a staged reduction step 210, the edge controller 106 compares the measured branch current from the branch current sensor 104 with the branch current limit." numerals.md: "| 210 | staged reduction step |"","fix":"In the paragraph at line 25 replace "This is the staged reduction of the allocation, and it is the first response of the edge controller 106 to a connector channel that is drawing more than it has been allowed." with "This is a first stage of the reduction of the allocation, continued at the staged reduction step 210 described below, and it is the first response of the edge controller 106 to a connector channel that is drawing more than it has been allowed.""},{"severity":"minor","category":"formalities","title":"Claim 5 requires the controller to operate a local fault indicator that the apparatus claim never recites","where":"draft/09-claims.md, claim 5 (line 25)","detail":"Claim 5 is an apparatus claim depending from claim 1. It recites a function performed on "a local fault indicator", but neither claim 1 nor claim 5 includes that indicator among the elements of the apparatus, so the claimed apparatus is required to operate a component it does not comprise. The parallel dependent claims in this set do it the other way and introduce their hardware properly — claim 8 "further comprising an isolated local bus", claim 9 "further comprising a network interface and a nonvolatile memory", claim 12 "further comprising a service input" — which shows the omission is a drafting slip rather than a deliberate scope choice. The element is fully described ("A fault indicator 116 is a local fault indicator at the installation and is operated by the processing unit 132"), so the correction needs no new matter. Method claim 17 is unaffected, because a method step may operate an indicator without reciting it as a component.","evidence":"Claim 5: "The apparatus of claim 1, wherein the controller is further configured to record a welded-contactor condition ... and, responsive to the recorded welded-contactor condition, to inhibit the contactors of the other connector channels of the plurality of connector channels and to operate a local fault indicator." Compare claim 8: "The apparatus of claim 1, further comprising an isolated local bus coupling the controller ..."","fix":"Recast claim 5 as: "The apparatus of claim 1, further comprising a local fault indicator, wherein the controller is further configured to record a welded-contactor condition when the connector current of a connector channel remains above a leakage threshold after an open command has been issued to the contactor of that connector channel, and, responsive to the recorded welded-contactor condition, to inhibit the contactors of the other connector channels of the plurality of connector channels and to operate the local fault indicator.""}]}
